# AI-Powered Internship Recommendation and Verification System

College major project. Follow this spec exactly; do not invent features or change architecture without explaining why first.

## Flow

Resume upload → SBERT semantic matching → cross-border filtering → ranked recommendations → suspicious-posting risk assessment → official company email verification → application → internship completion → blockchain-backed credential verification.

## Stack

- Frontend: React.js + Tailwind CSS + React Router + Axios
- Backend: Spring Boot REST API (Java), layered as controller / service / repository / entity / dto / security / config / exception / util
- Database: MySQL
- AI/NLP: separate Python microservice, Sentence-BERT (SBERT) + cosine similarity, exposing `/extract-resume`, `/embed`, `/match`
- Email: token/link-based verification via SMTP
- Blockchain: Ethereum-compatible network + Solidity smart contract (`issueCredential`, `verifyCredential`)
- Auth: JWT + role-based authorization (`STUDENT`, `COMPANY`, `ADMIN`)

## Architecture

Monorepo with four services. Keep AI and blockchain as separate services callable over HTTP/RPC — never inline SBERT or web3 calls into Spring Boot controllers.

```
internship-platform/
  frontend/
  backend/          # com.internship.platform
  ai-service/
  blockchain/
  docs/
```

## Database entities (core fields)

- users(id, name, email, password_hash, role, created_at)
- students(id, user_id, education, skills, projects, experience, certifications, interests, resume_path)
- companies(id, user_id, company_name, description, website, country, email, email_verified)
- student_preferences(id, student_id, country, location, work_mode, duration, minimum_stipend, currency, visa_required)
- internships(id, company_id, title, description, required_skills, country, city, work_mode, duration, stipend, currency, eligibility, visa_information, deadline, status)
- applications(id, student_id, internship_id, status[APPLIED/SHORTLISTED/ACCEPTED/REJECTED/COMPLETED], applied_at, updated_at)
- risk_assessments(id, internship_id, score, level, reasons, created_at)
- email_verifications(id, user_id NULLABLE, company_id NULLABLE, token, expiry, verified_at) — exactly one of user_id/company_id is set; enforce in the service layer
- credentials(id, credential_id, student_id, company_id, internship_id, completion_date, hash, blockchain_tx)
- blockchain_records(id, credential_id, transaction_hash, contract_address, network, timestamp)

Expand fields sensibly when implementing, but keep these cores.

## Key design rules (do not violate)

1. Recommendation order is always: mandatory structured filters (country / work-mode / duration / stipend / eligibility / visa) → SBERT semantic matching → cosine similarity → ranking. Never rank semantically before applying hard filters.

2. Cosine similarity is a ranking signal, not a probability. Never label it "% match to job success" or similar.

3. Risk assessment: weighted indicators (fee request=25, training/payment request=20, unrealistic stipend=15, guaranteed job promise=20, incomplete company info=10, suspicious payment instructions=20, duplicate posting=15). These sum to 125 — cap the total score at 100 before assigning Low(<40) / Medium(40-70) / High(>70) labels; state this cap explicitly in code comments. Never claim the score proves fraud — it is a warning signal only.

4. Email verification proves control of an inbox, not legal company identity. Do not imply otherwise in UI copy.

5. Keep the AI service and blockchain integration as separate services callable over HTTP/RPC. Never inline SBERT or web3 calls directly into Spring Boot controllers.

6. Never fabricate evaluation metrics anywhere (code comments, seed data, docs). Leave placeholders explicitly marked `TODO(measure after real evaluation)` rather than plausible-looking fake numbers.
